# watch-dmesg.sh writes one full ring-buffer dump per node per hour. A 72h soak
# is ~150 files and tens of MB; the fio JSON and bw logs beside them are the
# results worth keeping. Quote the decisive lines into documentations/16 instead.
results/dmesg-*.log
results/mounts-*.log
results/disks-*.log
results/watch.log
# fio writes an empty submission-latency log whenever the engine is synchronous.
results/*_slat.*.log
# fio's per-sample latency traces for the 72h soak: ~11 MB, and the JSON beside
# them already carries the full percentile distribution. The bandwidth logs are
# kept — they are the evidence that nothing degraded over three days.
results/*-soak_lat.*.log
results/*-soak_clat.*.log
# The soak's raw 10-second bandwidth samples: 415k lines for a claim that
# soak-summary.csv makes in 1.7k. Regenerate them by re-running the soak; the
# per-10-minute means are the evidence worth keeping in git.
results/*-soak_bw.*.log
